some bugs in your code are very  straightforward  you try running it you get a syntax  error somewhere python tells you exactly  where and you fix it this is great it's  simple and it's satisfying  sometimes though things crash and the  error is just kind of impossible to  understand this happens a lot in machine  learning for a few reasons you're  working with these big weird data  structures you're using these big  complex libraries with a lot of moving  parts  and also you're doing a lot of gpu  computing and that in general is much  more difficult to debug  in keras there's the additional problem  that your models are often compiled  before execution which is great for  performance but it makes debugging extra  difficult as well  so this is going to be a video about  what to do when you run into one of  those nightmare bugs and you just have  no idea where to begin with fixing it  so to give you some intuitions for  the the most common things that go wrong  and cause these weird issues  um and show you kind of where to look  for the sources of bugs that you  encounter let's use this example script  so i'll show it to you here in two parts  first we do all our imports we load a  data set we create a tokenizer and we  tokenize the data set  next we convert our data sets to  tensorflow data sets so that's  tf.data.dataset  and that's so that we can run fit on  them and then we load our model from a  pre-trained checkpoint we compile it and  we fit it with those data sets so this  seems straightforward enough it's  similar to what we've done in the course  before but beware this is spooky code  and hides many dark and mysterious  secrets  so what happens when we run it  well  it's not great so we get this error  message  but you know what does it mean we tried  to train on our data but we got no  gradient  it's it's  pretty perplexing i mean how do we even  begin to debug not getting a gradient so  when the error doesn't immediately  suggest where the problem is  the best solution is often to walk  through things in sequence making sure  at each stage that the outputs look  right that everything looks okay at that  point  and of course that means the place to  start is always to check your data  so the best way to make sure that the  data you're giving the model is good is  to grab a batch from the tf data data  set that your model is training on and  that that's because it's right at the  end of the training the data pipeline  and so that means that your if if those  outputs are good you're guaranteed that  your data pipeline is working well  so we can do that by looping over the  data set for one iteration and then  breaking and that gives us a single  batch  so what do we get when we inspect that  batch  we'll see that we're not getting any  gradient because we're not passing  labels to keras  so our labels are in the batch but  they're a key in the input dictionary  and they're not a separate label as  keras expects so this is one of the most  common issues you'll encounter when  training transformers models with  tensorflow our models can all compute  compute loss internally but to use that  loss for training the labels need to be  passed in the input dictionary where the  model can see them  this internal loss is the last that we  use when we don't specify a loss and  when we call compile when we don't  specify a loss argument  so keras on the other hand usually  expects labels to be passed separately  from the input dictionary and not to be  visible to the model and lost  computations will usually fail if you  don't do that  so we need to choose one or the other  either we use the model's internal loss  and we keep the labels where they are  or we keep using keras losses but we  move the labels to the place keras  expects them  so for simplicity here let's fix this  issue by using the model's internal loss  and we do that by removing the loss  argument from the call to compile  so  what happens if we try training now so  we recompile with that we call model.fit  again  what happens  well it does run this time but now we  get a loss of nan  so that's not good none means not a  number and it's not a good loss to have  in general  in fact if we inspect our model now  we'll see that not only are all the  outputs nan  all the weights are non as well as well  as the laws  so once a single man creeps into your  computations it tends to spread because  it propagates from the loss back and  once it's in the loss it's in the  gradient it gets to the gradient and  then once it's in the gradient it  enters the weight updates and then all  your weight updates end up as nan as  well so nan just completely destroyed  our model here but where did it creep in  first  so to find out we need to go back to a  point before the model was destroyed we  need to re-initialize the model and look  at the outputs for the very first batch  and when we do that we see that the nand  first appears in the loss but only in  some samples  so you can see this in more detail in  the accompanying section of the course  notes i am moving fairly quickly here  but we find that if we look at the  labels the samples with a loss of nan  all have a label value of two so this  gives us a very strong clue if we check  the model with  model.config.numlabels we see that the  model thinks there's only two labels but  if we see a label value of two that  means there's at least three labels  because of where zero is a label as well  so we got a loss of nand because we had  an impossible label in our data set and  to fix that we need to go back and set  the model to expect the right number of  labels so we can set num labels equals  three when we initialize the model with  from pre-trained and now hopefully we  can avoid this issue  so now we think our data is good and our  model is good and so training should  work but if we try running model.fit we  well  i mean the loss we do get a loss it is a  number and it is going down but it's not  going down very quickly and if we keep  running this out we'll find that it  stalls at a fairly high loss value so  what's going on  well when things are mostly working but  training is slow or a bit odd that can  often be a good time to look at your  optimizer and your training hyper  parameters  and this is where i want to mention one  of the most common sources of issues  when you're working with keras  you can  name things like optimizers with strings  so cara supports that and it's very  convenient but if you do that  all of the options get silently set to  their default values  so we specified our optimizer as adam  but in the process we invisibly got the  default learning rate which is 1e minus  3 or 10 to the power of -3  so this learning rate is way too high  for training transformer models we  should go back and specify the learning  rate directly not using a string so good  values here are between 1 e minus 5 and  1 e minus 4 so let's split the  difference and pick five e minus five  so if you recompile with that you'll  find that training actually works at  last the loss goes down efficiently and  it converges to a lower value  so again i did go through this quite  quickly and i strongly recommend  checking out the course notes to see  this in more detail and to experiment  with the code yourself and see what you  know what the errors look like and how  you can approach them  um but i've given you i hope i've given  you here a quick summary of  the most common bugs and maybe the most  common debris approaches to dealing with  them so good luck and remember to take  plenty of breaks if your code is giving  you a hard time
 

